1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,795
Pip has a plan. The wheels still wait.

2
00:00:02,965 --> 00:00:08,533
A sensor stream reaches our campus rover faster than its
computer can process it.

3
00:00:08,703 --> 00:00:15,573
A clever decision still needs memory, processing, connections,
and enough power to become an action.

4
00:00:15,743 --> 00:00:20,372
Have you ever wondered what gives an AI decision a physical
body?

5
00:00:20,542 --> 00:00:26,537
Chapter three, Hardware, will help us follow the whole path and
find the actual bottleneck.

6
00:00:28,846 --> 00:00:35,075
You will trace the sensing, memory, processing, and actuation
path of a small robot.

7
00:00:35,245 --> 00:00:42,285
You will explain why memory movement can limit a fast processor
and what a pipeline actually improves.

8
00:00:42,455 --> 00:00:49,452
You will also choose hardware by its job, rather than assuming
every AI system needs the same chip.

9
00:00:49,622 --> 00:00:54,828
Welcome to the tutorial on how hardware enables AI by Blue
Impulse.

10
00:00:54,998 --> 00:01:01,312
Follow each part of the diagram, then predict what happens when
that part becomes the bottleneck.

11
00:01:04,324 --> 00:01:11,727
The subtopic Hardware gives software a body groups hardware by
input, processing, output, and storage.

12
00:01:11,897 --> 00:01:20,025
For our robot, the sensor supplies observations. Memory holds
inputs, model parameters, and working results.

13
00:01:20,195 --> 00:01:26,957
The processor evaluates the program or model, and an output
communicates the resulting decision.

14
00:01:27,127 --> 00:01:33,101
If the robot needs to move, an actuator must turn that decision
into physical motion.

15
00:01:33,271 --> 00:01:40,759
Notice that one device can have multiple roles. A touchscreen
both displays output and senses input.

16
00:01:40,929 --> 00:01:48,374
The diagram is a chain of responsibilities, not a claim that
every box must be a separate physical chip.

17
00:01:52,324 --> 00:01:55,951
Use a kitchen to remember the memory hierarchy.

18
00:01:56,121 --> 00:01:59,641
Registers are like the tiny workspace immediately in your

19
00:01:59,751 --> 00:02:03,249
hands. Cache keeps useful ingredients close to the cook.

20
00:02:03,419 --> 00:02:10,182
Main memory is the larger active workbench, while persistent
storage is more like the pantry.

21
00:02:10,352 --> 00:02:16,325
The analogy is about access and capacity, not a literal map of
computer components.

22
00:02:16,495 --> 00:02:24,431
A huge pantry does not help if ingredients arrive too slowly.
Similarly, a processor can wait for data.

23
00:02:24,601 --> 00:02:32,409
Capacity asks how much fits. Memory bandwidth asks how quickly
data can move. These are different questions.

24
00:02:35,628 --> 00:02:39,404
Model weights, inputs, and intermediate results must fit

25
00:02:39,514 --> 00:02:43,397
somewhere. A processor cannot use data that has not arrived.

26
00:02:43,567 --> 00:02:46,020
When memory bandwidth is insufficient,

27
00:02:46,130 --> 00:02:51,485
a fast processor may spend time waiting instead of doing useful
arithmetic.

28
00:02:51,655 --> 00:02:58,375
Adding storage capacity alone does not automatically remove a
memory-transfer bottleneck.

29
00:02:58,545 --> 00:03:02,492
The table also distinguishes cache, main RAM,

30
00:03:02,602 --> 00:03:07,807
and secondary storage. They are not interchangeable names for
one resource.

31
00:03:07,977 --> 00:03:15,828
Both static RAM and dynamic RAM are volatile, although their
circuits and refresh requirements differ.

32
00:03:15,998 --> 00:03:19,326
For this tutorial, focus on the system question:

33
00:03:19,436 --> 00:03:23,745
is the limiting factor how much fits, or how quickly it arrives?

34
00:03:23,915 --> 00:03:29,056
Imagine the processor as a cook waiting for ingredients. Hiring
a faster

35
00:03:29,166 --> 00:03:33,881
cook does not shorten the time a missing ingredient spends in
transit.

36
00:03:34,051 --> 00:03:38,254
Likewise, a faster arithmetic unit can still spend time waiting

37
00:03:38,364 --> 00:03:42,268
for memory. Measure that waiting before choosing the upgrade.

38
00:03:47,628 --> 00:03:52,386
Now compare the processor roles in the subtopic Hardware for AI.

39
00:03:52,556 --> 00:03:56,097
A CPU handles general control, branches,

40
00:03:56,207 --> 00:04:02,522
and diverse tasks. A GPU can accelerate suitable parallel
arithmetic workloads.

41
00:04:02,692 --> 00:04:08,132
Specialised accelerators can efficiently perform supported
tensor operations,

42
00:04:08,242 --> 00:04:11,314
but their benefits depend on the actual workload.

43
00:04:11,484 --> 00:04:20,764
Simple tabular reinforcement learning can run on an ordinary
CPU. AI does not always require a GPU.

44
00:04:20,934 --> 00:04:27,462
Training changes model parameters. Inference uses an existing
model to produce an output.

45
00:04:27,632 --> 00:04:35,440
A model can be trained on a large server and deployed on a
smaller device if the deployment requirements fit.

46
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:45,510
Use laundry to understand pipelining. One load can dry while a
later load is washing.

47
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:51,333
In our simplified processor, the stages are fetch, decode,
execute, and write.

48
00:04:51,503 --> 00:04:59,290
Instruction one enters fetch in cycle one. In cycle two, it
decodes while instruction two enters fetch.

49
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:03,918
Keep stepping forward. The instructions occupy different stages,

50
00:05:04,028 --> 00:05:07,911
so several instructions make progress during the same cycle.

51
00:05:08,081 --> 00:05:15,825
After the pipeline fills, this ideal example completes one
instruction per cycle, assuming no stalls.

52
00:05:15,995 --> 00:05:22,950
It does not make an individual four-stage instruction take just
one cycle from start to finish.

53
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:27,856
Real pipelines can pause for dependencies, uncertain branches,

54
00:05:27,966 --> 00:05:32,680
or shared resources. These hazards limit the ideal improvement.

55
00:05:32,850 --> 00:05:40,360
Use the included Python table to compare completion cycles
rather than relying only on the moving picture.

56
00:05:45,541 --> 00:05:50,043
Trace the environmental control loop: measurement becomes a
signal,

57
00:05:50,153 --> 00:05:55,081
processing chooses an output, and an actuator changes the
environment.

58
00:05:55,251 --> 00:06:03,507
An analogue sensor may need an analogue-to-digital converter.
Many sensors already provide digital measurements.

59
00:06:03,677 --> 00:06:10,738
The processor evaluates a decision, then a driver or power stage
supplies what the actuator needs.

60
00:06:10,908 --> 00:06:16,156
Some actuators use a digital interface or pulse-width
modulation.

61
00:06:16,266 --> 00:06:20,149
They do not all require a digital-to-analogue converter.

62
00:06:20,319 --> 00:06:28,511
Pulse-width modulation is timed switching, not the same thing as
converting a value into an analogue voltage.

63
00:06:28,681 --> 00:06:35,379
A control signal can tell a motor what to do without being able
to supply the current that moves it.

64
00:06:35,549 --> 00:06:40,200
Pip's control signal communicates what the motor driver should
do. The

65
00:06:40,310 --> 00:06:44,918
energy that turns a loaded wheel still comes through the power
system.

66
00:06:49,715 --> 00:06:56,862
Imagine the robot detects an obstacle, selects a turn, and sends
a command to its motor driver.

67
00:06:57,032 --> 00:07:03,475
The driver powers the motor, the wheels move, and the sensor
measures the changed situation.

68
00:07:03,645 --> 00:07:10,535
That final measurement closes the feedback loop. The command
alone does not prove the movement succeeded.

69
00:07:10,705 --> 00:07:17,191
A wheel might slip, a sensor might be noisy, or the actuator
might reach a mechanical limit.

70
00:07:17,361 --> 00:07:24,785
Monitoring and control differ. Displaying a measurement
monitors; changing the environment adds control.

71
00:07:24,955 --> 00:07:31,888
This loop can use a fixed rule or a learned policy. The hardware
path alone does not tell you which.

72
00:07:35,628 --> 00:07:40,940
Pause and diagnose a system. Its processor repeatedly waits for
data,

73
00:07:41,050 --> 00:07:44,272
and its motor driver cannot supply enough current.

74
00:07:44,442 --> 00:07:51,887
Would replacing the processor with a faster GPU solve both
problems? Think before the answer appears.

75
00:07:58,057 --> 00:08:06,057
No. Memory movement and actuator power are separate limits that
a new GPU does not automatically remove.

76
00:08:06,227 --> 00:08:12,371
Measure the path, identify the limiting stage, and match the
intervention to that evidence.

77
00:08:12,541 --> 00:08:18,728
Chapter six, Strengths and Limitations, adds power, heat,
memory, and physical constraints.

78
00:08:18,898 --> 00:08:23,719
An advertised arithmetic rate describes one capability. It is

79
00:08:23,829 --> 00:08:27,946
not a complete measurement of the robot's useful performance.

80
00:08:32,498 --> 00:08:37,682
Trace Pip's whole path: observations enter, memory holds
information,

81
00:08:37,792 --> 00:08:41,952
processing evaluates it, and outputs communicate or act.

82
00:08:42,122 --> 00:08:49,780
Memory capacity and bandwidth both matter. Pipeline throughput
is different from the latency of one instruction.

83
00:08:49,950 --> 00:08:57,182
Conversion, control signals, actuator power, and processor
strengths all affect what the system can do.

84
00:08:57,352 --> 00:09:02,536
Choose hardware by the workload, deadlines, power budget, and
physical task.

85
00:09:02,706 --> 00:09:07,144
To check your understanding, draw a robot's path from camera to
wheel,

86
00:09:07,254 --> 00:09:10,688
and label one possible bottleneck at each stage.

87
00:09:10,858 --> 00:09:16,832
Then explain why a more powerful chip does not, by itself,
create a learning algorithm.

88
00:09:17,002 --> 00:09:22,335
An upgrade should target the constrained part of the task. Check
the input rate,

89
00:09:22,445 --> 00:09:27,800
memory traffic, compute load, and actuator response as a
connected system.

90
00:09:32,498 --> 00:09:36,252
Pip's small problem now has an explanation you can test.

91
00:09:36,422 --> 00:09:41,841
Use Chapter 3, Hardware, and the named subtopics to revisit the
reasoning.

92
00:09:42,011 --> 00:09:45,424
The tutorial guide includes the full teaching steps

93
00:09:45,534 --> 00:09:51,294
and the checked practice answers. Run the supporting
demonstration and change one input.

94
00:09:51,464 --> 00:09:56,798
Blue Impulse. Follow the signal, predict the result, and check
your explanation.
